Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 10:58:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(cli): stop `run` silently dropping piped stdin, add
 --prompt/--prompt-file
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`atomic-agent run` is a chat loop where one stdin line is one user message.
Two things made it unusable from a script:

1. **Buffered lines were discarded.** `rl.question` consumes one line and
   readline drains the rest of the chunk while that first turn is still in
   the model — with no `line` listener attached, those lines went nowhere.
   `printf 'a\nb\nc\n' | atomic-agent run` ran turn `a` and silently threw
   away `b` and `c`. Verified against a 6-line brief: 1 turn before, 5
   after. A `pending` queue fed by a `line` listener restores the
   documented contract. The line-synchronised
   `eval-memory/harness/multi-turn-driver.ts` is unaffected — it writes one
   line per reply, so it never has a line in flight to queue.

2. **No way to send a multi-line message at all.** Any newline starts a new
   turn, so piping a multi-line brief makes turn 0 the first line alone and
   the model answers a fragment ("the message looks cut off"). `--prompt
   <text>` and `--prompt-file <path|->` seed the *first* user message
   intact.

Per invariant #6 these flags are not a one-shot goal mode: the seed goes
through the same `runtime.runTurn` as any other message and the stdin loop
keeps reading afterwards, so a TTY drops into interactive chat and a pipe
ends at EOF.

Also fixes `run --help`, which exited 2 with `unknown flag: --help` — the
one place an operator would have found the line-per-turn contract
documented.

`src/cli/run-agent.ts` had no test coverage; adds 22 tests, including a
fake-runtime chat loop that asserts the exact message text of every turn.
